Task 1: Credit Scoring Model

CodeAlpha Machine Learning Internship

Objective

Predict whether an applicant is likely to be creditworthy (1) or not creditworthy (0) using historical financial and profile information.

Features

• Income, debt, age, late payments, employment type and credit-history length.
• Logistic Regression, Decision Tree and Random Forest options.
• Preprocessing for missing values, categorical columns and numeric scaling.
• Accuracy, Precision, Recall, F1-score, ROC-AUC and confusion matrix.
• Saves the trained pipeline to models/credit_scoring_model.joblib.

Run locally

python -m venv .venv
# Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
# macOS/Linux: source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt

# Creates a synthetic demo CSV if no dataset exists, then trains Random Forest
python main.py --make-demo-data

# Try the other classifiers
python main.py --model logistic_regression
python main.py --model decision_tree

Use your own dataset

Place a CSV at data/credit_data.csv or run:

python main.py --data path/to/your_file.csv --model random_forest

The CSV must have a target column named creditworthy (0 = not creditworthy, 1 = creditworthy). All other columns are treated as input features. The code automatically detects numeric and categorical columns.

Dataset note

The included demo-data generator creates synthetic data only to demonstrate the pipeline. It is not real customer data and its metrics must not be presented as real-world credit-risk performance. For a proper study, use a documented public credit dataset (for example, the UCI Statlog German Credit dataset), check its target-label meaning, and adapt the target column to creditworthy.

